{
  "image_name": "0ffda54df0933ae76384d2d0826298bf.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how *Chlamydia* interacts with epithelial cells and nearby immune cells, and how these responses differ between ordinary infection and the invasive LGV form. In epithelial cells, the bacteria are shown cycling between small elementary bodies and larger reticulate bodies inside an inclusion. One inset at the top suggests the pathogen manipulates host gene transcription through phosphorylated STAT1 and suppression of MYC, while another pathway links tryptophan catabolism by IDO to restriction of bacterial growth. JAK/STAT and IFNγ signaling are highlighted around the infected epithelium.\n\nBelow, the image contrasts responses in neutrophils and macrophages. Neutrophils appear to sense released bacterial products through FPR2, activate granules and the inflammasome, generate ROS, and undergo pyroptosis. Macrophages take up bacteria, including LGV, into autolysosome-like compartments; ROS, GBP proteins, and the inflammasome are again emphasized, leading to pyroptosis and cytokine release. CPAF and a labeled CT135 factor appear to modulate these host defenses. The right side suggests innate immune cells and cytokines contribute to IFNγ production, and surviving LGV organisms may persist in macrophages and drive reinfection of epithelial cells. Overall, the figure depicts intracellular development, immune evasion, and inflammatory cell death during chlamydial infection.",
